In PHP, inheritance contract refers to methods that must be overridden with the same signature/visibility. When a parent class declares a method as public or protected, if a child class overrides it, PHP has certain requirements about visibility (the child's method must not be less visible than the parent's method).

However, private methods are NOT part of the inheritance contract. A private method in the parent class is not accessible from the child class. When a child class declares a method with the same name as a private method in the parent class, it is NOT considered an override. Instead, the child class's method is a completely separate method.

